(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-coronary-artery-disease-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Coronary Artery Disease Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 61-year-old man with an acute myocardial infarction is taken for coronary angiography. Which vessel is most often the culprit in this setting?","choices":["Anterior interventricular artery","Posterior interventricular branch","Right coronary artery","Left circumflex artery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Occlusion of the anterior interventricular artery, formerly called the left anterior descending artery, is the most common cause of acute myocardial infarction (about 40–50%), followed by the right coronary artery and then the left circumflex. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with an inferior ST-elevation myocardial infarction suddenly develops complete heart block on the monitor. Which artery is most likely occluded?","choices":["Diagonal artery","Right coronary artery","Left circumflex artery","Anterior interventricular artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The right coronary artery supplies the atrioventricular node, so an occlusion that produces complete heart block points to it. That is why right coronary artery infarcts are the ones most likely to cause dysrhythmias. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman with chest pressure has a first high-sensitivity troponin I of 8 ng/L and a second, drawn later, of 30 ng/L. How should the second result be interpreted?","choices":["A lab error to ignore","A normal result for a woman","A stable, reassuring result","A rising, abnormal result"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Serial troponins are drawn to watch the change. Her second high-sensitivity troponin I of 30 ng/L is above the women's 99th percentile of 12 ng/L and higher than the first, so another is ordered as cells continue to die. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with long-standing diabetes and peripheral neuropathy has had two days of shortness of breath, nausea and fatigue, with no chest pain. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Treat as viral gastroenteritis","Reassure her and send her home","Obtain a stat electrocardiogram","Order an outpatient stress test"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chest discomfort may be absent in silent ischemia, which occurs with diabetes, peripheral neuropathy and a transplanted heart. Dyspnea, nausea and fatigue can be the presentation, and every chest pain workup starts with a stat electrocardiogram. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 29, 30, 32"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with a non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction is on aspirin, clopidogrel and heparin before catheterization. A colleague suggests adding eptifibatide. Which antiplatelet class, having fallen out of favor, should not be added?","choices":["Glycoprotein 2b/3a inhibitor","P2Y12 receptor antagonist","Aspirin, the cyclooxygenase inhibitor","High-intensity statin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Eptifibatide, abciximab and tirofiban block the glycoprotein 2b/3a receptor, and this class has fallen out of favor. Antithrombotic therapy is typically dual antiplatelet therapy with or without anticoagulation. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 52, 74"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with chest pain in the emergency department is being risk-stratified with the HEART score. Which element does this score include?","choices":["Chest radiograph","Calcium score","D-dimer level","Initial troponin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The HEART score is built from History, Electrocardiogram, Age, Risk factors and the initial Troponin. It predicts the risk of an adverse outcome in acute coronary syndrome. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has chest pressure when he climbs two flights of stairs. It fades after three minutes of rest and has followed the same pattern for months. His resting electrocardiogram is normal. Which test is most appropriate next?","choices":["Serial troponin I measurements","Ergonovine provocation test","Posterior-lead electrocardiogram","Exercise treadmill stress test"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stable angina is diagnosed by stress-test-induced electrocardiogram changes, usually T-wave inversion, on an exercise (treadmill), nuclear or cardiac computed tomography stress test. Coronary angiography remains the gold standard. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 37, 38"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man has chest pressure radiating to his left shoulder, with shortness of breath and sweating, while shoveling snow. Which treatment typically relieves this type of pain?","choices":["An antacid","A muscle relaxant","An anti-inflammatory","Nitroglycerin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Typical cardiac chest pain is substernal pressure, worse with exertion, radiating to the neck or shoulder, with shortness of breath and diaphoresis, and relieved with nitroglycerin. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man describes his chest pressure while holding a clenched fist over his sternum (Levine sign). Which condition does this gesture most suggest?","choices":["Aortic dissection","Biliary colic","Pancreatitis","Myocardial ischemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The Levine sign, a clenched fist held over the sternum while describing the discomfort, is the classic gesture of ischemic, anginal chest pain. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has chest pain with ST elevation in V1 through V4. Which artery is the most likely culprit?","choices":["Right coronary artery trunk","Anterior interventricular artery","Posterior interventricular branch","Left circumflex coronary artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. V1-V4 are the anterior and septal leads, supplied by the anterior interventricular artery, formerly the left anterior descending artery, the most common culprit in acute infarction. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 61"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with an ST-elevation myocardial infarction is to receive a fibrinolytic because the nearest intervention unit is far away. Which agent is the most commonly used today?","choices":["Tenecteplase","Streptokinase","Eptifibatide","Heparin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tenecteplase is the most common fibrinolytic today. Alteplase, reteplase, streptokinase, anistreplase and lanoteplase are other agents, and every one carries a risk of intracerebral hemorrhage. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man with six hours of atypical chest discomfort has a normal electrocardiogram and two high-sensitivity troponin I values of 4 ng/L. Which disposition is most appropriate?","choices":["Start thrombolytic therapy","Admit to intensive care","Discharge with follow-up","Urgent cardiac catheterization"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Troponin is a tool that says who can safely go home. Two high-sensitivity troponin I values of 4 ng/L, far below the men's 20 ng/L, with a normal electrocardiogram rule out an infarct. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man has 30 minutes of crushing chest pressure. His electrocardiogram shows a new left bundle branch block; a tracing last month was normal. How should this be managed?","choices":["As stable angina","As unstable angina","As a benign conduction delay","As an ST-elevation infarct"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A new left bundle branch block with ischemic symptoms is treated as an ST-elevation myocardial infarction equivalent: aspirin and activation of the catheterization team without waiting. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with stable exertional angina has a documented contraindication to beta blockers. Which medication class is used instead?","choices":["Thrombolytic agent","Phosphodiesterase inhibitor","Calcium channel blocker","Nonselective beta blocker"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In stable angina, a calcium channel blocker takes the place of the beta blocker when beta blockers are contraindicated, alongside lifestyle change, a statin, aspirin and sublingual nitroglycerin. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with unstable angina undergoes catheterization. Her narrowed coronary artery is dilated with an inflated balloon, and no stent is left behind. Which term describes this procedure?","choices":["Atherectomy","Plain old balloon angioplasty","Percutaneous coronary intervention","Coronary angiography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Plain old balloon angioplasty uses a balloon to open a narrowed artery without placing a stent. Once a drug-eluting or bare stent is placed, the procedure is called a percutaneous coronary intervention. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 50, 69"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has ST elevation in leads II, III and aVF and in the right-sided lead V4R, consistent with right ventricular infarction. His blood pressure is 104/66 mmHg. Which medication should be avoided?","choices":["A heparin drip","Nitroglycerin","Chewable aspirin","Clopidogrel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In a right ventricular infarct, do not give nitroglycerin: it lowers preload, the failing right ventricle loses its Frank-Starling support, output falls further and the patient can go into shock. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 62, 63"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man at a hospital with a catheterization lab has ST elevation in leads I, aVL, V5 and V6. He has chewed his aspirin. Which team is called now?","choices":["Outpatient cardiology clinic","Inpatient medicine team","Cardiac catheterization team","Cardiothoracic surgery team"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of an ST-elevation infarction is aspirin and a call to the cardiac catheterization (percutaneous coronary intervention) team, with a door-to-balloon goal under 90 minutes. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 66, 68"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man has an anterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction at a rural hospital. The nearest percutaneous coronary intervention unit is three hours away. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Coronary artery bypass graft","Thrombolytic within 30 minutes","Transfer without a thrombolytic","Wait for a second troponin I"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When the closest percutaneous intervention unit is two or more hours away, thrombolytics are given, started within 30 minutes of arrival, after checking for contraindications. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 67, 74"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman is admitted with unstable angina. Her oxygen saturation (SpO2) is 98% on room air. Which part of the classic initial bundle should be withheld?","choices":["Supplemental oxygen","Chewable aspirin","A beta blocker","Clopidogrel"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oxygen is given only when the oxygen saturation is below 91%; with normal oxygenation it is not administered because it causes peripheral vasoconstriction. The rest of the bundle stands. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 49, 73"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with a non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction is stabilized and taken to the catheterization laboratory. Dye shows a tight narrowing of the anterior interventricular artery, which is opened with a balloon and held open with a drug-eluting stent. Which term names the procedure that opened the artery?","choices":["Plain old balloon angioplasty","Percutaneous coronary intervention","Coronary angiography","Left heart catheterization"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Percutaneous coronary intervention is the placement of a drug-eluting or non-drug-eluting stent to keep the coronary artery open. Anything done to the vessel during the catheterization, beyond imaging it, is the intervention. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 50, 51"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman who smokes has recurrent chest pain at rest between 3 and 5 a.m., each episode lasting about 10 minutes. An electrocardiogram during one episode shows transient ST-segment elevation that resolves, and coronary angiography shows no significant stenosis. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Vasospastic angina","Unstable angina","Stable angina","ST-elevation myocardial infarction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vasospastic (Prinzmetal) angina occurs at rest, most often between midnight and early morning, in younger patients who smoke, with transient ST-segment elevation during an episode and no high-grade stenosis. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 88, 89, 90"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman has an echocardiogram two days after an anterior myocardial infarction. One segment of the left ventricular wall bulges outward when the rest of the ventricle contracts. Which term describes this wall motion?","choices":["Hypokinesia","Hyperkinesia","Dyskinesia","Akinesia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dyskinesia is bulging, paradoxical wall movement. It is one of the three regional wall motion abnormalities an echocardiogram shows after ischemia, alongside hypokinesia and akinesia. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man admitted with a non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction has a TIMI (Thrombolysis in Myocardial Infarction) risk score of 5. Which management does this score support?","choices":["A repeat score before deciding","An early invasive approach","Discharge with outpatient follow-up","Medical therapy with no angiography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A TIMI score of 3 or higher marks the patient who needs intensive care or emergent revascularization, so a score of 5 justifies an early invasive approach with catheterization. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 71, 72"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with stable angina is prescribed sublingual nitroglycerin. What should he do if his chest pain is unimproved five minutes after one dose?","choices":["Call emergency medical services","Lie down and wait an hour","Double his daily beta blocker","Book a clinic visit this week"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sublingual nitroglycerin is the cornerstone of angina therapy, but pain that is unimproved or worse five minutes after one dose, or not relieved by three doses, means calling emergency medical services. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 39, 40"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man who smokes reports brief episodes of chest pain at rest in the early morning that resolve on their own. Coronary vasospasm is suspected. Which test is the gold standard for confirming the diagnosis?","choices":["Angiography with a provocation agent","Treadmill stress electrocardiogram","Nuclear perfusion stress imaging","Coronary calcium scoring by computed tomography"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The gold standard is coronary artery spasm provoked during angiography by ergonovine or acetylcholine, then resolved with nitroglycerin given through the catheter. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with known stable angina says her chest pain now comes more often, lasts longer and appears with less exertion. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Outpatient stress test next month","Add a nightly nitrate patch","Emergency department evaluation","Routine follow-up in 3 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A change in a patient's stable angina (occurring at rest, new and limiting, or increasing in frequency and discomfort) is unstable angina, an acute coronary syndrome and a medical emergency. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 45, 49"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man with no known heart disease collapses while jogging and cannot be resuscitated. Autopsy shows extensive coronary artery disease. What is the most likely cause of his sudden cardiac death?","choices":["Electrolyte abnormality","Complete heart block","Ventricular fibrillation","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The most common cause of sudden cardiac death is ventricular fibrillation, and sudden death is the initial manifestation of coronary artery disease in over 20% of patients, as it was for him. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has recurrent anginal chest pain during periods of work stress and anxiety. Her electrocardiogram shows ST changes during symptoms, and coronary angiography shows normal coronary arteries. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Unstable angina","Microvascular angina","Vasospastic angina","Stable angina"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Microvascular angina (formerly cardiac syndrome X) is angina with ST changes, possibly with elevated biomarkers, but normal coronary arteries on the angiogram; it is more frequent in women and often associated with stress or anxiety. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 93, 94"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with hypertension, obesity and a sedentary office job asks which of his coronary risk factors he cannot change. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["His age and sex","His blood pressure","His body weight","His activity level"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Age, sex and genetics (family history) are the nonmodifiable coronary risk factors. Hypertension, obesity and a sedentary lifestyle are all modifiable and are where his effort should go. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 23"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic stable angina asks about preventing future attacks. Which platelet-inhibiting regimen is more effective than either drug alone?","choices":["Aspirin plus warfarin","Clopidogrel plus ticagrelor","Aspirin plus a heparin drip","Aspirin plus clopidogrel"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin (used in chronic stable angina and after a survived acute coronary syndrome) and clopidogrel both inhibit platelets, and together they are more effective than either one alone. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 41"}]};
